            "sampleAnswer": "Mobile phones—and particularly smartphones—have transformed communication in ways that are so pervasive that it's now difficult to remember what communication looked like before them. The most fundamental change is probably the shift from synchronous to asynchronous communication as the default mode—before mobile phones, most communication was either face-to-face or by phone call, both of which require both parties to be available at the same time. Messaging has made it normal to communicate in a way that doesn't require immediate response, which has both advantages—you can communicate at your own pace, across time zones, without interrupting what you're doing—and disadvantages—the expectation of availability has in some ways increased rather than decreased, and many people feel a pressure to respond quickly that can be genuinely stressful. The range of communication modes available has also expanded dramatically—text, voice, video, image, and emoji all serve different communicative functions and have created new forms of expression. I think one of the most significant changes is the blurring of the boundary between public and private communication—the ease with which messages can be shared, forwarded, and broadcast has changed the nature of private conversation in ways that have significant implications for trust and intimacy. The permanence of digital communication—the fact that messages can be stored, searched, and retrieved—is also a significant change from the ephemeral nature of spoken conversation.",
