            "sampleAnswer": "Mobile phones offer significant potential benefits for elderly people, but also present genuine challenges, and I think the balance between benefits and challenges varies considerably depending on the individual and the level of support available to them. The most significant advantage is probably safety and connection—for elderly people living alone, a mobile phone provides a direct link to family, friends, and emergency services that can be genuinely life-saving. The ability to call for help in an emergency, to stay in contact with family members, and to access information and services remotely has real and significant value. Video calling in particular has been transformative for many elderly people—the ability to see as well as hear family members who live at a distance has reduced the isolation that can be a significant problem in later life. The disadvantages are also real—the complexity of modern smartphones can be genuinely daunting for people who didn't grow up with digital technology, and the pace of change means that interfaces and apps are constantly evolving in ways that require continuous learning. The risk of fraud and scams is also disproportionately high for elderly users, who are often specifically targeted by criminals. I think the most important thing is to ensure that elderly people have access to the support and training they need to use phones safely and effectively, and that phone design takes the needs of older users seriously rather than treating them as an afterthought.",
